 ],
 "caption": "An orange with a straw. That was the whole brand.\n\nIn 2009 Tropicana redesigned its carton. The orange with the straw went; a glass of juice came in, and the arched logo turned into a vertical wordmark. Clean, modern, and hard to find. Shoppers walked past it. Unit sales fell 20% between January 1 and February 22 (IRI data, reported by Bloomberg Businessweek), and the old carton was back within two months.\n\nBahlsen's 2021 redesign won a D&AD award. It played down the biscuit photos and dropped the familiar blue. The CEO later said sales fell 11.8% in the first five months of 2023, and in 2025 the blue and the clear product shots came back.\n\nMy take: the lesson isn't \"never redesign\". Research by William Caruso at the Ehrenberg-Bass Institute (2026, household shoppers in the US and UK) says a redesign works when it looks fresher without erasing the cues people recognise: colour, logo, layout. His advice is to modernise only when the pack's likeability has measurably dropped.\n\nThe limits: inflation also hit Bahlsen's sales, so the design isn't the whole story. The university release doesn't give the study's sample size. Sources: Bloomberg Businessweek, Adelaide University (Ehrenberg-Bass Institute), Creative Bloq, Lebensmittel Praxis, The Branding Journal. Pack images: Tropicana (via The Branding Journal) and Bahlsen; other objects are AI-generated.\n\nWhat do people actually recognise you by: a colour, a shape, a picture? And would it survive your next redesign? Write to me.\n\n— Behnood Kavian",
 "hashtags": [
  "#branding",
